  46:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** 
  47:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** /**
  48:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * @defgroup ComplexFFT Complex FFT Functions
  49:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *
  50:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \par
  51:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * The Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) is an efficient algorithm for computing the
  52:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * Discrete Fourier Transform (DFT).  The FFT can be orders of magnitude faster
  53:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * than the DFT, especially for long lengths.
  54:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * The algorithms described in this section
  55:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * operate on complex data.  A separate set of functions is devoted to handling
  56:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * of real sequences.
  57:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \par
  58:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * There are separate algorithms for handling floating-point, Q15, and Q31 data
  59:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * types.  The algorithms available for each data type are described next.
  60:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \par
  61:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * The FFT functions operate in-place.  That is, the array holding the input data
  62:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * will also be used to hold the corresponding result.  The input data is complex
  63:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * and contains <code>2*fftLen</code> interleaved values as shown below.
  64:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * <pre> {real[0], imag[0], real[1], imag[1],..} </pre>
  65:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * The FFT result will be contained in the same array and the frequency domain
  66:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * values will have the same interleaving.
  67:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *
  68:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \par Floating-point
  69:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * The floating-point complex FFT uses a mixed-radix algorithm.  Multiple radix-8
  70:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * stages are performed along with a single radix-2 or radix-4 stage, as needed.
  71:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * The algorithm supports lengths of [16, 32, 64, ..., 4096] and each length uses
  72:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * a different twiddle factor table.
  73:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \par
  74:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * The function uses the standard FFT definition and output values may grow by a
  75:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * factor of <code>fftLen</code> when computing the forward transform.  The
  76:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * inverse transform includes a scale of <code>1/fftLen</code> as part of the
  77:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * calculation and this matches the textbook definition of the inverse FFT.
  78:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \par
  79:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * Pre-initialized data structures containing twiddle factors and bit reversal
  80:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * tables are provided and defined in <code>arm_const_structs.h</code>.  Include
  81:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * this header in your function and then pass one of the constant structures as
  82:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * an argument to arm_cfft_f32.  For example:
  83:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \par
  84:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * <code>arm_cfft_f32(arm_cfft_sR_f32_len64, pSrc, 1, 1)</code>
  85:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \par
ARM GAS  C:\Users\Lamp\AppData\Local\Temp\ccAunX2S.s 			page 3


  86:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * computes a 64-point inverse complex FFT including bit reversal.
  87:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * The data structures are treated as constant data and not modified during the
  88:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * calculation.  The same data structure can be reused for multiple transforms
  89:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * including mixing forward and inverse transforms.
  90:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \par
  91:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * Earlier releases of the library provided separate radix-2 and radix-4
  92:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * algorithms that operated on floating-point data.  These functions are still
  93:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * provided but are deprecated.  The older functions are slower and less general
  94:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * than the new functions.
  95:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \par
  96:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * An example of initialization of the constants for the arm_cfft_f32 function follows:
  97:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \code
  98:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * const static arm_cfft_instance_f32 *S;
  99:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * ...
 100:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *   switch (length) {
 101:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *     case 16:
 102:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       S = &arm_cfft_sR_f32_len16;
 103:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       break;
 104:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *     case 32:
 105:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       S = &arm_cfft_sR_f32_len32;
 106:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       break;
 107:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *     case 64:
 108:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       S = &arm_cfft_sR_f32_len64;
 109:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       break;
 110:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *     case 128:
 111:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       S = &arm_cfft_sR_f32_len128;
 112:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       break;
 113:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *     case 256:
 114:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       S = &arm_cfft_sR_f32_len256;
 115:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       break;
 116:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *     case 512:
 117:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       S = &arm_cfft_sR_f32_len512;
 118:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       break;
 119:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *     case 1024:
 120:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       S = &arm_cfft_sR_f32_len1024;
 121:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       break;
 122:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *     case 2048:
 123:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       S = &arm_cfft_sR_f32_len2048;
 124:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       break;
 125:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *     case 4096:
 126:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       S = &arm_cfft_sR_f32_len4096;
 127:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *       break;
 128:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** *   }
 129:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \endcode
 130:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \par Q15 and Q31
 131:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * The floating-point complex FFT uses a mixed-radix algorithm.  Multiple radix-4
 132:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * stages are performed along with a single radix-2 stage, as needed.
 133:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * The algorithm supports lengths of [16, 32, 64, ..., 4096] and each length uses
 134:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * a different twiddle factor table.
 135:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \par
 136:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * The function uses the standard FFT definition and output values may grow by a
 137:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * factor of <code>fftLen</code> when computing the forward transform.  The
 138:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * inverse transform includes a scale of <code>1/fftLen</code> as part of the
 139:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * calculation and this matches the textbook definition of the inverse FFT.
 140:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \par
 141:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * Pre-initialized data structures containing twiddle factors and bit reversal
 142:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * tables are provided and defined in <code>arm_const_structs.h</code>.  Include
ARM GAS  C:\Users\Lamp\AppData\Local\Temp\ccAunX2S.s 			page 4


 143:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * this header in your function and then pass one of the constant structures as
 144:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * an argument to arm_cfft_q31.  For example:
 145:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \par
 146:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * <code>arm_cfft_q31(arm_cfft_sR_q31_len64, pSrc, 1, 1)</code>
 147:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/TransformFunctions/arm_cfft_f32.c **** * \par
